**Claim 1.** A method for processing a force sensor signal to generate haptic feedback and to control menu scrolling, the method comprising:

receiving, at a processing circuit, a force signal from a force sensor disposed on a device, the force signal being representative of a magnitude of an applied force exerted upon a surface associated with the force sensor;

sampling the force signal at a predetermined sampling rate to obtain a plurality of successive force values;

comparing at least one of the plurality of successive force values to a first force threshold;

responsive to determining that the at least one of the plurality of successive force values equals or exceeds the first force threshold, initiating a timing measurement that measures an elapsed duration during which the force signal continuously satisfies the first force threshold;

comparing the elapsed duration to a first time threshold and to a second time threshold, the second time threshold being greater than the first time threshold;

responsive to determining that the elapsed duration satisfies the first time threshold but does not satisfy the second time threshold, generating a first control signal that causes a haptic actuator of the device to output a first haptic feedback output, and generating a menu control signal that causes a menu displayed on the device to scroll by a first scroll increment;

responsive to determining that the elapsed duration satisfies the second time threshold, generating a second control signal that causes the haptic actuator to output a second haptic feedback output distinct from the first haptic feedback output, and generating a further menu control signal that causes the menu to scroll by a second scroll increment different from the first scroll increment;

comparing the at least one of the plurality of successive force values to a second force threshold that is greater than the first force threshold; and

responsive to determining that the at least one of the plurality of successive force values equals or exceeds the second force threshold, adjusting a rate of the scrolling of the menu as a function of the magnitude of the applied force represented by the force signal, such that the rate of the scrolling increases as the magnitude of the applied force increases.

---

You may wish to add dependent claims to strengthen the set. Representative examples follow:

**Claim 2.** The method of claim 1, further comprising applying a low-pass filter to the force signal prior to said comparing the at least one of the plurality of successive force values to the first force threshold, so as to attenuate transient noise in the force signal.

**Claim 3.** The method of claim 1, wherein the first haptic feedback output comprises a haptic pulse of a first amplitude and the second haptic feedback output comprises a haptic pulse of a second amplitude greater than the first amplitude.

**Claim 4.** The method of claim 1, further comprising, responsive to determining that the force signal ceases to satisfy the first force threshold, terminating the timing measurement and inhibiting further scrolling of the menu.

**Claim 5.** The method of claim 1, wherein adjusting the rate of the scrolling comprises mapping the magnitude of the applied force to the rate of the scrolling in accordance with a monotonically increasing transfer function.

**Claim 6.** The method of claim 1, further comprising generating a discrete haptic detent output each time the menu scrolls past a respective menu item during the scrolling.

If you let me know the specific device context (for example, a wearable, a vehicle control, or a stylus), the number of force thresholds, or whether the haptic outputs should be tied to particular menu boundaries, I can tailor the independent claim and expand the dependent claim set accordingly.
